    {
      "answer": "Self-RAG does not simply retrieve passages for every input. It learns to **retrieve on demand**, generate using the retrieved passages, and apply fine-grained self-reflection to judge passage relevance and whether its own output is supported [2][3][4].\n\nWhen retrieval is used at test time, performance improves substantially compared with no retrieval: in the reported ablation, \u201cNo retrieval\u201d scores **24.7, 73.0**, whereas retrieving the top passage scores **41.8, 73.1, and 28.6** across the listed metrics [1]. However, always imposing retrieval constraints is not optimal: \u201cHard constraints\u201d scores **28.3 and 72.6**, while the full SELF-RAG model reaches **45.5, 73.5, and 32.1** [1]. This suggests that SELF-RAG benefits from **adaptive retrieval plus self-reflection**, rather than retrieval being forced uniformly.\n\nSELF-RAG also filters irrelevant passages through self-reflection and evaluates factual support, without requiring external models at inference [3]. Removing the support-reflection component (\u201cRemove ISSUP\u201d) lowers performance relative to the full model in the reported results [1].",
